WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.349 --> 00:00:10.521 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to Canada's provinces, part 1 of 1. First, I clear the chosen names
for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:11.183 --> 00:00:21.249 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
We work through 13 of the Canada regions the map names, once each: where it
sits, one fact worth keeping, and a hook to remember it by.

3
00:00:21.923 --> 00:00:24.686 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Northwest Territories into view.

4
00:00:31.583 --> 00:00:50.303 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Northwest Territories is one of Canada’s three territories, not a
province, and it fills the far north-west of the country, reaching the Arctic
Ocean to the north and Alaska to the west, with Yellowknife on the shore of
Great Slave Lake.

5
00:00:50.957 --> 00:01:08.057 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It has eleven official languages and nine of them are Indigenous, more than
any other jurisdiction in the country, and it has been carved up again and
again, with Alberta, Saskatchewan, Yukon and Nunavut all taken out of it.

6
00:01:08.708 --> 00:01:22.315 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the far north-west, where the forest stops and the tundra runs to the
Arctic Ocean, and eleven languages are spoken side by side in one town.

7
00:01:23.011 --> 00:01:25.263 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Nunavut into view.

8
00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:50.076 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Nunavut is one of Canada’s three territories, not a province, and it covers
the whole centre-north of the country, reaching the Arctic Ocean in the north
and Hudson Bay in the south, and touching Québec in the east and Manitoba in
the south, with Iqaluit on Frobisher Bay off Baffin Island.

9
00:01:50.727 --> 00:02:07.647 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It is the youngest and largest jurisdiction in Canada, created in 1999 and
named by the people who live there, our land in Inuktitut, with about 40,000
people and no road connecting it to the rest of the country.

10
00:02:08.304 --> 00:02:17.940 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember our land, the newest region on the map, where in midsummer the sun
sets for only a few hours and no road reaches it.

11
00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:21.093 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Yukon into view.

12
00:02:25.925 --> 00:02:40.728 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Yukon is one of Canada’s three territories, not a province, and it lies in the
far west of the country, touching Alaska to the west and the Beaufort Sea to
the north, with Whitehorse in the south.

13
00:02:41.387 --> 00:03:02.663 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name comes from a Gwich’in word for great river, the Yukon River, which
runs 3,190 kilometres to the Bering Sea, and the Klondike gold rush of 1897
brought about 100,000 people north.

14
00:03:03.327 --> 00:03:14.751 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the white horse country of the great river running to the Bering Sea,
with Mount Logan, Canada’s highest peak, standing over the icefields to the
east.

15
00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.906 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Manitoba into view.

16
00:03:24.009 --> 00:03:36.109 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Manitoba is a province, not a territory, and it stands in the middle of the
country, running north to Hudson Bay and south to the border with the United
States, with Winnipeg in the middle.

17
00:03:36.764 --> 00:03:53.972 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name comes from Cree or Ojibwe words for the strait of the spirit, the
sound the waves make on the rocks of Lake Manitoba, and Churchill on its
Hudson Bay coast calls itself the polar bear capital of the world.

18
00:03:54.626 --> 00:04:02.126 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the strait of the spirit, the province with about a hundred thousand
lakes, where polar bears walk through the town of Churchill.

19
00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.072 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Saskatchewan into view.

20
00:04:11.261 --> 00:04:27.029 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Saskatchewan is a province, not a territory, and it is the prairie strip
between Alberta and Manitoba, touching the American border to the south and
the Northwest Territories and Nunavut to the north, with Saskatoon in the
middle.

21
00:04:27.686 --> 00:04:36.510 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name comes from Cree words for swift-flowing river, and it supplies a
large share of the world’s potash and uranium, both mined from under the
prairie.

22
00:04:37.174 --> 00:04:45.568 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the nearly rectangular province, flat enough to see the grain
elevators for miles, sitting on top of the world’s potash.

23
00:04:46.284 --> 00:04:48.281 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Alberta into view.

24
00:04:57.428 --> 00:05:10.048 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Alberta is a province, not a territory, and it lies in the west between
British Columbia and Saskatchewan, with its southern edge on the border with
the United States and Calgary near the middle.

25
00:05:10.712 --> 00:05:23.587 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It was named in 1882 for Princess Louise Caroline Alberta, Queen Victoria’s
daughter, whose husband was then governor general, and its oil sands hold one
of the largest crude reserves on earth.

26
00:05:24.239 --> 00:05:35.245 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the province that is one vast oil field under the prairie, with the
country’s first national park and the dinosaur beds of the badlands in the
same province.

27
00:05:35.972 --> 00:05:38.352 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring British Columbia into view.

28
00:05:45.131 --> 00:06:00.719 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
British Columbia is a province, not a territory, and it forms the whole
Pacific coast of Canada, running from Alaska and Yukon in the north down to
the American border in the south, with Vancouver on the coast.

29
00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:17.354 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Queen Victoria chose the name, Columbia for the Columbia River and British to
tell it apart from the American side, and once the fjords and islands are
counted the province has more than 25,000 kilometres of coastline.

30
00:06:18.012 --> 00:06:28.287 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the misty Pacific province, where the temperate rainforest grows
trees over 90 metres tall and Vancouver is the mildest big city in the
country.

31
00:06:29.042 --> 00:06:31.260 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Québec into view.

32
00:06:37.287 --> 00:06:53.091 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Québec is a province, not a territory, and it lies in the east of the country,
running from the American border in the south up the St Lawrence River to the
Atlantic coast, with Montréal in the south and Québec City at the narrowing of
the river.

33
00:06:53.750 --> 00:07:05.430 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
French is the only official language in this province and about 85 per cent of
people speak it at home, and the province also generates almost all of its
electricity from water.

34
00:07:06.088 --> 00:07:16.363 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember where the river narrows, the French-speaking province on the St
Lawrence, whose towns run from the American border up to the Atlantic sea.

35
00:07:17.039 --> 00:07:20.452 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Newfoundland and Labrador into view.

36
00:07:24.264 --> 00:07:39.564 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Newfoundland and Labrador is a province, not a territory, and it sits on the
Atlantic in the far east of Canada, the island of Newfoundland and the coast
of Labrador opposite it, with St. John’s at the south-east corner of the
island.

37
00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:58.014 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
L’Anse aux Meadows here is the only confirmed Norse site in North America,
five hundred years before Columbus, and the cod fishery that was the richest
in the world for four centuries collapsed in 1992, when 30,000 people lost
their work in a day.

38
00:07:58.676 --> 00:08:11.134 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the new found land of 1497, with Viking turf houses above the bay and
the cod shoals that made the region rich fished out.

39
00:08:11.821 --> 00:08:14.456 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Ontario into view.

40
00:08:19.214 --> 00:08:32.066 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Ontario is a province, not a territory, and it fills the centre-east of the
country, from the border with the United States in the south up to Hudson Bay,
with Toronto on Lake Ontario and Québec to the east.

41
00:08:32.731 --> 00:08:45.351 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name comes from an Iroquoian word for great lake or beautiful water, and
the border with the United States runs through four of the five Great Lakes,
with Niagara Falls on it.

42
00:08:46.014 --> 00:08:53.816 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the province of great lakes, where nearly two in five Canadians live
and the American border runs straight through the water.

43
00:08:54.503 --> 00:08:58.172 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Prince Edward Island into view.

44
00:09:01.722 --> 00:09:16.950 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Prince Edward Island is a province, not a territory, and it is a small island
in the Gulf of St Lawrence off the coast of Nova Scotia, with Charlottetown in
the middle and no land border with any other region.

45
00:09:17.601 --> 00:09:32.439 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its soil is red from iron oxide and the island grows a quarter of Canada’s
potatoes, and at about 5,700 square kilometres it is the smallest province and
the most densely populated.

46
00:09:33.089 --> 00:09:42.389 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the smallest province, the red-soil island where the farmland is one
field of potatoes and one story about a girl with red hair.

47
00:09:43.066 --> 00:09:46.177 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring New Brunswick into view.

48
00:09:50.583 --> 00:10:08.187 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
New Brunswick is a province, not a territory, and it lies on the Atlantic in
the east, its southern edge on the border with the United States, between
Québec to the north and Nova Scotia to the south-west, with Moncton in the
south-east.

49
00:10:08.837 --> 00:10:21.550 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It is the only officially bilingual province, with about a third of it Acadian
French-speaking, and the Bay of Fundy on its coast has the highest tides on
earth, up to 16 metres.

50
00:10:22.206 --> 00:10:31.459 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the province where the water rises and falls the highest in the world
twice a day, and where English and French share the towns.

51
00:10:32.132 --> 00:10:35.673 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Nova Scotia into view.

52
00:10:39.799 --> 00:10:59.563 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Nova Scotia is a province, not a territory, and it lies on the Atlantic in the
far east of the country, facing the American states of Maine and Massachusetts
across the Gulf of Maine, with Halifax on its coast and only New Brunswick
sharing a land border with it.

53
00:11:00.221 --> 00:11:19.337 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name is Latin for New Scotland, from a 1621 land grant by James VI of
Scotland, and Halifax harbour saw the largest man-made explosion before the
atomic bomb when a munitions ship blew up in 1917.

54
00:11:19.994 --> 00:11:30.188 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember New Scotland, joined to the mainland by a strip only 24 kilometres
wide, with no point on it more than 67 kilometres from the sea.

55
00:11:36.437 --> 00:11:58.613 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
13 regions of Canada, one for each target of the map: Northwest Territories,
Nunavut, Yukon, Manitoba, Saskatchewan, Alberta, British Columbia, Québec,
Newfoundland and Labrador, Ontario, Prince Edward Island, New Brunswick and
Nova Scotia.

56
00:11:59.263 --> 00:12:09.108 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the shape of Canada from the inside: the regions nest around each
other, and every name on the map belongs somewhere in this one country.
